</code></pre> <p>At <code>factor: 1.0</code> one simulated second takes one real second. The run stops at simulation time 20 (<code>until: 20</code>), so it takes 20 real seconds. Each record is one line: <code>[EVT]</code> lines are events, and <code>[TEL]</code> lines are telemetry. The only telemetry here is <code>system.simulation.lag_seconds</code>, published once per simulation second, which says how far the run is behind the wall clock. These are the events of the first 6 seconds:</p> <pre><code>[EVT] {'sim_ts': 2.552, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-1', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 0, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'status': 'queued'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 2.552, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-1', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 0, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'status': 'started'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 2.696, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-2', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 1, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'status': 'queued'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 3.737, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-3', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 2, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'status': 'queued'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 4.02, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-4', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 3, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'status': 'queued'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 4.052, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-1', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 0, 'value': {'event_type': 'order_packed'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 4.052, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-2', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 1, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'status': 'started'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 5.552, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-2', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 1, 'value': {'event_type': 'order_packed'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 5.552, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-3', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 2, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'status': 'started'}}
</code></pre> <p>Read <code>sim_ts</code>, the simulation time of each record. It does not count up in small steps: it jumps from 2.552 to 2.696, then to 3.737, 4.02 and 4.052. The simulation clock moves straight to the time of the next event, because nothing changes between two events. At <code>factor: 0</code> the same run takes a fraction of a second, with the same records.</p> <p><code>--sample 5</code> shows the first records without waiting and without changing the file. It runs the blueprint unpaced, as fast as it can, prints its first 5 events to stdout as JSON lines (one record per line), and stops:</p> <pre><code>desflow run first_shop.yaml --sample 5
</code></pre> <pre><code>{"stream_type":"event","sim_ts":2.552,"timestamp":"...","key":"orders-1","task":"pack_order","instance":0,"value":{"path_id":"Shop.service.packing","status":"queued"}}
{"stream_type":"event","sim_ts":2.552,"timestamp":"...","key":"orders-1","task":"pack_order","instance":0,"value":{"path_id":"Shop.service.packing","status":"started"}}
{"stream_type":"event","sim_ts":2.696,"timestamp":"...","key":"orders-2","task":"pack_order","instance":1,"value":{"path_id":"Shop.service.packing","status":"queued"}}
{"stream_type":"event","sim_ts":3.737,"timestamp":"...","key":"orders-3","task":"pack_order","instance":2,"value":{"path_id":"Shop.service.packing","status":"queued"}}
{"stream_type":"event","sim_ts":4.02,"timestamp":"...","key":"orders-4","task":"pack_order","instance":3,"value":{"path_id":"Shop.service.packing","status":"queued"}}
</code></pre> <p>They are the first five events above, each printed as the whole record, with its <code>stream_type</code>. The run's log stays on stderr, so a pipe such as <code>| jq .key</code> reads only the records. YAML Blueprints lists the other ways to run a blueprint.</p> <p><code>desflow</code> on its own lists every command, grouped by what it does, with examples. <code>desflow run --help</code> lists the options of <code>run</code>, also with examples. Command Line uses every command on one shop.</p>

Full files <p>The Builder and Low-level files count the outcomes with <code>_Outcomes</code>, a sink of their own. Its <code>run</code> method takes each batch of records from the sink's queue and counts the filled and cancelled orders by size. Egress describes how every sink takes its records.</p> YAMLBuilderLow-level docs/snippets/yaml/filtered_inventory.yaml<pre><code># The Filtered Inventory tutorial: each order waits for a shirt of its own size.
